18|\title{\textbf{Weight-Space Anatomy and Zero-Training LoRA Surgery:\\Decoupling Representation Clustering from Readout Drift in On-Policy Self-Distillation}}
19|\author{Thomas Li \\ Microsoft Edge AI}
20|\date{November 3, 2026}
21|
22|\begin{document}
23|\maketitle
24|
25|\begin{abstract}
26|When adapting Small Language Models (SLMs) toward a frontier teacher on structured multi-entity generation tasks such as Browser Tab Grouping, practitioners face a sharp trade-off between off-policy Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) and On-Policy Self-Distillation (OPSD). On \texttt{Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B} (32 layers, 21.2M LoRA parameters across 248 modules), training on 1,200 frontier (\texttt{GPT-6-astra}) reference solutions via off-policy SFT yields strong fine-grained sub-task splitting on complex sessions (\texttt{v2.1}), but causes a catastrophic \textbf{$-8.33\text{pp}$ to $-9.90\text{pp}$ Recall collapse} on standard sessions (\texttt{v1}). Conversely, OPSD preserves \texttt{v1} Recall ($72.52\%$ Run-1 / $70.20\%$ 3-run) while fixing giant-bucket over-merging, yet splits complex multi-task sessions less aggressively than SFT.
27|
28|Inspired by recent advances in \emph{Weight-Space Programming} (Takehi et al., 2026), we perform a full 32-layer QR-SVD and unembedding-projection anatomy of the effective LoRA weight updates $\Delta W = W - W_0$ without running gradients. We uncover an exact structural bifurcation at \textbf{Layer 18}: (1) middle layers (\textbf{L08--L15}) learn a shared cross-tab clustering circuit ($\cos(\Delta W_{\text{OPSD}}, \Delta W_{\text{SFT}}) \text{ up to } 0.37$), whereas (2) late layers (\textbf{L18--L31}) become nearly orthogonal ($\cos \text{ down to } 0.05$), where off-policy SFT's top singular directions project $8\times$--$11\times$ more strongly onto surface lexical artifacts (\texttt{' \&'}) and aggressive \texttt{Ungrouped} rejection thresholds. Armed with this white-box map, we demonstrate two families of \textbf{zero-training weight-space surgery}: (i) \emph{Single-Model Neuron/Scalar Edits} (Ideas A, B, C), where scaling just 64 newline-vs-comma MLP columns per layer in L26--L31 of the base SFT model lifts \texttt{v2.1} Recall from $45.71\%$ to $57.93\%$ ($+12.22\text{pp}$) with zero training and without touching OPSD; and (ii) \emph{Dual-Adapter Circuit Grafting \& Spectral Consensus} (\texttt{Graft-18} and \texttt{SVD-Blend-50/50}), which push \texttt{v2.1} Recall to \textbf{$64.52\%$} ($+5.16\text{pp}$ over OPSD) and Initial Precision to \textbf{$72.41\%$}, surpassing both parent paradigms.
29|\end{abstract}
30|
31|\section{Introduction}
32|Post-training compact language models (2B--4B parameters) for structured clustering and multi-entity organization requires balancing two distinct capabilities: \emph{internal relational representation} (distinguishing fine-grained sub-tasks across 20--35 browser tabs) and \emph{calibrated readout policy} (deciding when to emit a newline \texttt{\textbackslash n} to start a new group versus a comma \texttt{,} to continue merging, or when to assign a tab to \texttt{Ungrouped}).
33|
34|Standard off-policy SFT applies token-level cross-entropy under teacher forcing on frontier labels $y^\star$. Because group ordering and naming are permutation-invariant, teacher forcing penalizes the student heavily for surface naming preferences (e.g., compound labels containing \texttt{' \& '}) and shifts its rejection threshold. On-Policy Self-Distillation (OPSD) resolves this exposure bias by sampling rollouts $\hat{y} \sim p_S(\cdot \mid x)$ from the student and querying a frozen copy of the student conditioned on privileged context $[x, y^\star]$ to provide token-level Generalized Jensen-Shannon Divergence (JSD) supervision along the student's own trajectory. However, why off-policy SFT and on-policy OPSD exhibit opposite strengths across simple (\texttt{v1}) and complex (\texttt{v2.1}) regimes—and whether their respective flaws can be repaired directly in weight space without retraining—has remained an open mechanistic question.
35|
36|\section{Related Work}
37|\paragraph{On-Policy Distillation.} Generalized Knowledge Distillation (GKD; Agarwal et al., 2024) and MiniLLM (Gu et al., 2024) train students on self-generated rollouts using white-box teacher logits. Self-Distilled Reasoner / OPSD (Zhao et al., 2026) extends on-policy distillation to settings where the true teacher is a closed-source frontier model by conditioning a white-box teacher on privileged reference answers $y^\star$.
38|\paragraph{Weight-Space Programming and Mechanistic Surgery.} Takehi et al. (2026) recently demonstrated that frontier LLMs can directly inspect neural network weights and perform targeted weight-space edits (such as ablating 64 looping MLP neurons in \texttt{Qwen3.5-0.8B}) without gradient descent, while noting that weight edits can remove obstacles hiding existing capabilities (``unlocking'') rather than instilling complex distributed capabilities from scratch. Our work bridges these two lines: we use exact low-rank SVD and unembedding projections on 32-layer LoRA adapters to turn weight-space analysis into reproducible zero-training surgical algorithms.
39|
40|\section{Weight-Space Anatomy of LoRA Updates}
41|
42|\subsection{Architecture and Exact Low-Rank SVD Formulation}
43|We study \texttt{Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B}, a 32-layer hybrid architecture interleaving 24 Gated-DeltaNet linear-attention layers with 8 full-attention layers (at layers $3, 7, 11, 15, 19, 23, 27, 31$), with residual dimension $d_{\text{model}} = 2560$ and FFN intermediate dimension $d_{\text{ff}} = 9216$. Each LoRA adapter ($r=16, \alpha=32$, scaling factor $s = \alpha/r = 2.0$) attaches 248 low-rank pairs $(A_m \in \mathbb{R}^{16 \times d_{\text{in}}}, B_m \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}} \times 16})$, totaling $21,233,664$ float32 parameters ($82.9\text{ MB}$).
44|
45|Starting from the audited 2000-step SFT checkpoint $W_0$ (\texttt{sft2000}), both \texttt{opsd\_astra} and \texttt{sft\_astra} are trained for exactly 150 steps ($\text{grad\_accum}=8$, 1,200 prompts labeled by \texttt{GPT-6-astra}). To avoid gauge ambiguity in $(A,B)$ factor space, we analyze the exact effective weight displacement relative to $W_0$:
46|\begin{equation}
47|\Delta W_m = s(B_{1,m} A_{1,m} - B_{0,m} A_{0,m}) = s \begin{bmatrix} B_{1,m} & -B_{0,m} \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} A_{1,m} \\ A_{0,m} \end{bmatrix}.
48|\end{equation}
49|Via reduced QR factorizations $Q_B R_B = s[B_{1,m}, -B_{0,m}]$ and $Q_A R_A = [A_{1,m}^\top, A_{0,m}^\top]$, the SVD of the full $d_{\text{out}} \times d_{\text{in}}$ matrix reduces exactly to a $32 \times 32$ core SVD $R_B R_A^\top = \tilde{U} \Sigma \tilde{V}^\top$, yielding exact left/right singular vectors $U = Q_B \tilde{U}$ and $V = Q_A \tilde{V}$. For residual-writing modules (\texttt{mlp.down\_proj}, \texttt{self\_attn.o\_proj}, \texttt{linear\_attn.out\_proj}) where $d_{\text{out}} = 2560$, we project $\Delta W_m$ and its leading left singular vector $u_1$ onto the RMSNorm-weighted unembedding matrix $U_{\text{eff}} = W_{\text{embed}} \odot \gamma_{\text{norm}} \in \mathbb{R}^{248320 \times 2560}$.
50|
51|\begin{figure}[t]
52|\centering
53|\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_weight_space_heatmap.pdf}
54|\caption{\textbf{32-Layer Weight-Space Anatomy of \texttt{Qwen3.5-4B} LoRA Updates.} \textbf{(A)} Frobenius cosine similarity $\cos(\Delta W_{\text{OPSD}}, \Delta W_{\text{SFT}})$ peaks in middle layers L08--L15 ($0.34$--$0.37$) and collapses after Layer 18 (down to $0.0517$ at L26 \texttt{mlp.down\_proj}). \textbf{(B)} Unembedding projection ratio $\|v^\top \Delta W_{\text{SFT}}\|_2 / \|v^\top \Delta W_{\text{OPSD}}\|_2$ reveals an $8\times$--$11\times$ surge in late-layer writing toward the compound naming token \texttt{' \&'} (ID 594) and \texttt{Ungrouped} under off-policy SFT. \textbf{(C)} Layerwise update norm $\|\Delta W\|_F$ showing SFT's $1.4\times$--$1.7\times$ magnitude expansion.}
55|\label{fig:heatmap}
56|\end{figure}
57|
58|\subsection{Three Empirical Discoveries from the 32-Layer Heatmap}
59|Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} presents the complete 32-layer weight-space diagnosis:
60|\begin{enumerate}
61|    \item \textbf{Middle-Layer Sub-Task Clustering Resonance (L08--L15):} Across layers 8 to 15 (anchored by full-attention hubs L07, L11, and L15), $\Delta W_{\text{OPSD}}$ and $\Delta W_{\text{SFT}}$ align strongly, reaching $\cos = 0.3665$ at L12 and $0.3487$ at L11 \texttt{mlp.down\_proj}. Both training paradigms modify the same middle-layer relational circuits to separate co-occurring sub-tasks (e.g., separating \emph{Luggage} from \emph{San Francisco Airbnb} within travel tabs). Because SFT applies unclipped cross-entropy on every token, its middle-layer update norm is $1.54\times$--$1.71\times$ larger than OPSD's (Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap}C), driving finer splitting on \texttt{v2.1}.
62|    \item \textbf{Late-Layer Orthogonal Divergence (L18--L31):} Starting sharply at Layer 18, directional alignment drops to $\cos \in [0.0517, 0.15]$. At L26 \texttt{mlp.down\_proj} ($\cos = 0.0517$), less than $0.27\%$ of SFT's update energy lies along OPSD's direction.
63|    \item \textbf{Direct Localization of the \texttt{v1} Recall Collapse:} Decoding the top-1 left singular vector $u_1$ of $\Delta W_{\text{SFT}}$ through $U_{\text{eff}}$ at L18--L31 exposes almost exclusively \texttt{[' \&', '\&', ' learning', ' Learning']} (Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap}B). Auditing the 1,200 Astra training labels confirms that $13.03\%$ of Astra group names contain \texttt{' \& '} (vs.\ $1.43\%$ in original SFT data, a $9.1\times$ shift) and $15.3\%$ more tabs are labeled \texttt{Ungrouped}. Teacher-forced SFT expends massive late-layer capacity memorizing Astra's lexical style and elevated \texttt{Ungrouped} threshold—dissolving valid 2-tab groups on \texttt{v1} (increasing \texttt{v1} ungrouped tabs from 238 to 324)—whereas OPSD's on-policy trajectory evaluation filters out this surface distribution drift ($0.7\%$ \texttt{\&}-groups).
64|\end{enumerate}
65|
66|\section{Experiments: Zero-Training Weight-Space Surgery}
67|
68|We evaluate two surgical paradigms on the frozen 61-case Run-1 benchmark (45 \texttt{v1} Golden45 cases + 16 \texttt{v2.1} hard multi-task cases) on a single NVIDIA A100-80GB in exact float32 precision.
69|
70|\begin{figure}[t]
71|\centering
72|\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig2_neurons_and_pareto.pdf}
73|\caption{\textbf{Single-Neuron Readout Polarity and Benchmark Pareto Frontier.} \textbf{(A)} Projection of individual intermediate neuron columns in L31 \texttt{mlp.down\_proj} ($[2560, 9216]$) onto the newline-vs-comma direction $v_{\texttt{\textbackslash n}} - v_{\texttt{,}}$. \textbf{(B)} Run-1 exact Recall frontier on \texttt{v1} vs.\ \texttt{v2.1}: both single-model weight edits (Ideas A, B, C) and dual-adapter surgeries (\texttt{Graft-18}, \texttt{SVD-Blend}) substantially improve over their parent baselines with \textbf{zero training steps}.}
74|\label{fig:pareto}
75|\end{figure}
76|
77|\subsection{Paradigm I: Single-Model Value \& Neuron Edits (No Second Adapter Used)}
78|To test pure Weight-Space Programming in the strict sense of Takehi et al. (2026)—fixing a single model by directly editing specific weights or scalars without using a second trained adapter—we evaluate three targeted interventions:
79|\begin{itemize}
80|    \item \textbf{Idea A (64-Neuron Split/Merge Valve Edit on Base \texttt{sft2000}):} Giant-bucket over-merging is ultimately a readout race between emitting a newline \texttt{\textbackslash n} (Token 198, starting a new group) versus a comma \texttt{,} (Token 11, appending another tab ID to the current bucket). For each layer $\ell \in \{26,\dots,31\}$, we project all $9,216$ columns of the base \texttt{mlp.down\_proj} weight matrix onto $v_{\text{split}} = \hat{u}_{198} - \hat{u}_{11}$ (Figure~\ref{fig:pareto}A). Scaling the top-64 newline-promoting columns by $2.0\times$ and zeroing ($0.0\times$) the top-64 comma-promoting columns ($0.69\%$ of columns) in \texttt{sft2000}—with \textbf{zero training and no access to Astra or OPSD}—lifts \texttt{v2.1} Recall from \textbf{$45.71\%$ to $57.93\%$ ($+12.22\text{pp}$)} and \texttt{v1} Recall from $68.26\%$ to $69.70\%$ ($+1.44\text{pp}$)!
81|    \item \textbf{Idea B (Single-Scalar Singular Value Excision $\sigma_1, \sigma_2 \to 0$ on \texttt{sft\_astra}):} Without touching OPSD, we take $\Delta W_{\text{SFT}} = U \operatorname{diag}(\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_{16}) V^\top$ in layers 18--31 and simply set the top-1 (\textbf{Idea B-1}, 1 scalar per module) or top-2 (\textbf{Idea B-2}, 2 scalars per module) singular values to $0.0$, removing the lexical/threshold drift mode while keeping modes $3\dots 16$ intact. Idea B-2 recovers \textbf{$+3.44\text{pp}$ Recall} ($64.19\% \to 67.63\%$) and $+1.11\text{pp}$ Precision on \texttt{v1} while preserving $60.25\%$ Recall ($66.13\%$ Precision) on \texttt{v2.1}.
82|    \item \textbf{Idea C (4-Scalar Full-Attention \texttt{q\_norm} Temperature Scaling on \texttt{sft2000}):} Multiplying \texttt{self\_attn.q\_norm} by a single constant $1.35\times$ across the 4 middle full-attention layers (L07, L11, L15, L19) sharpens cross-tab attention, lifting \texttt{v1} Recall to \textbf{$70.37\%$ ($+2.11\text{pp}$)} and \texttt{v2.1} Recall to $51.96\%$ ($+6.25\text{pp}$).
83|\end{itemize}
84|
