94|\subsection{Paradigm I: Single-Model Value \& Neuron Edits (No Second Adapter Used)}
95|To test pure Weight-Space Programming in the strict sense of Takehi et al. (2026)—fixing a single model by directly editing specific weights or scalars without using a second trained adapter—we evaluate three targeted interventions:
96|\begin{itemize}
97|    \item \textbf{Idea A (64-Neuron Split/Merge Valve Edit on Base \texttt{sft2000}):} Giant-bucket over-merging is ultimately a readout race between emitting a newline \texttt{\textbackslash n} (Token 198, starting a new group) versus a comma \texttt{,} (Token 11, appending another tab ID to the current bucket). For each layer $\ell \in \{26,\dots,31\}$, we project all $9,216$ columns of the base \texttt{mlp.down\_proj} weight matrix onto $v_{\text{split}} = \hat{u}_{198} - \hat{u}_{11}$ (Figure~\ref{fig:pareto}A). Scaling the top-64 newline-promoting columns by $2.0\times$ and zeroing ($0.0\times$) the top-64 comma-promoting columns ($0.69\%$ of columns) in \texttt{sft2000}—with \textbf{zero training and no access to Astra or OPSD}—lifts \texttt{v2.1} Recall from \textbf{$45.71\%$ to $57.93\%$ ($+12.22\text{pp}$)} and \texttt{v1} Recall from $68.26\%$ to $69.70\%$ ($+1.44\text{pp}$)!
98|    \item \textbf{Idea B (Single-Scalar Singular Value Excision $\sigma_1, \sigma_2 \to 0$ on \texttt{sft\_astra}):} Without touching OPSD, we take $\Delta W_{\text{SFT}} = U \operatorname{diag}(\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_{16}) V^\top$ in layers 18--31 and simply set the top-1 (\textbf{Idea B-1}, 1 scalar per module) or top-2 (\textbf{Idea B-2}, 2 scalars per module) singular values to $0.0$, removing the lexical/threshold drift mode while keeping modes $3\dots 16$ intact. Idea B-2 recovers \textbf{$+3.44\text{pp}$ Recall} ($64.19\% \to 67.63\%$) and $+1.11\text{pp}$ Precision on \texttt{v1} while preserving $60.25\%$ Recall ($66.13\%$ Precision) on \texttt{v2.1}.
99|    \item \textbf{Idea C (4-Scalar Full-Attention \texttt{q\_norm} Temperature Scaling on \texttt{sft2000}):} Multiplying \texttt{self\_attn.q\_norm} by a single constant $1.35\times$ across the 4 middle full-attention layers (L07, L11, L15, L19) sharpens cross-tab attention, lifting \texttt{v1} Recall to \textbf{$70.37\%$ ($+2.11\text{pp}$)} and \texttt{v2.1} Recall to $51.96\%$ ($+6.25\text{pp}$).
100|\end{itemize}
