133|\subsection{Paradigm II: Dual-Adapter Circuit Grafting \& Spectral Consensus}
134|To push absolute task performance beyond what either training paradigm achieves alone, we combine middle-layer SFT representations with OPSD readout stability (Table~\ref{tab:main}):
135|\begin{enumerate}
136|    \item \textbf{Surgery A (\texttt{Graft-18}: Layerwise Circuit Grafting at L18):} Copying $(A_m, B_m)$ from \texttt{sft\_astra} for layers $0\dots 17$ and from \texttt{opsd\_astra} for layers $18\dots 31$ recovers $+5.00\text{pp}$ Recall on \texttt{v1} ($64.19\% \to 69.19\%$) while jumping to \textbf{$64.52\%$ Recall on \texttt{v2.1}}—exceeding both \texttt{opsd\_astra} ($59.36\%$) and \texttt{sft\_astra} ($60.25\%$). Sweeping the cut point across L14, L18, and L22 traces an inverted-U curve peaking exactly at L18, while the reverse control (\texttt{Rev-Graft-18}) immediately drops \texttt{v1} Recall by $-5.04\text{pp}$, proving causally that SFT's damage is localized to L18--L31.
137|    \item \textbf{Surgery B (\texttt{SVD-Blend-50/50}: Rank-16 Spectral Consensus Algorithm):} Rather than choosing a layer boundary manually, we compute the exact rank-16 SVD truncation of the effective matrix average across all 248 modules:
138|    \begin{equation}
139|    \widehat{W}_m = \operatorname{SVD}_{16}\!\left(\frac{1}{2} B_{\text{OPSD},m} A_{\text{OPSD},m} + \frac{1}{2} B_{\text{SFT},m} A_{\text{SFT},m}\right).
140|    \end{equation}
141|    Because shared middle-layer directions ($\cos \approx 0.36$) interfere constructively while orthogonal late-layer artifacts ($\cos \approx 0.05$) are halved and truncated by $\operatorname{SVD}_{16}$, \texttt{SVD-Blend-50/50} achieves the \textbf{highest \texttt{v2.1} Initial Precision ($72.41\%$, $+7.33\text{pp}$ over SFT)} alongside \textbf{$64.42\%$ Recall}.
142|\end{enumerate}
143|
144|\subsection{Case-Level Verification}
145|\begin{itemize}
146|    \item \textbf{\texttt{v2.1 / JH02} (25 tabs across Soccer Balls, Grok AI, Manchester City, Data Analysis):} Base \texttt{sft2000} collapses all 25 tabs into a single \texttt{Soccer: 1..25} bucket ($0\%$ Recall). \textbf{Idea A} (editing 64 columns in L26--L31 of \texttt{sft2000} with zero training) cleanly separates \texttt{Amazon: 1,6,17,23,25 | Grok: 2,3,4,11,13,14,20 | Manchester City: 5,8,9,10,12,16,19 | Data Analysis: 7,18,21,22} ($75.0\%$ P, $60.0\%$ R).
147|    \item \textbf{\texttt{v2.1 / JH16} (13 tabs):} Base \texttt{sft2000} merges non-Minecraft tabs into one coarse \texttt{Education} group ($33.3\%$ R), while \texttt{sft\_astra} drops \texttt{Elyon Christian School: 6, 9} into \texttt{Ungrouped}. \textbf{Idea A} achieves \textbf{$100.0\%$ Precision and $100.0\%$ Recall}, and both \textbf{Idea B-2} ($\sigma_1=\sigma_2=0$) and \textbf{\texttt{Graft-18}} rescue \texttt{Elyon Christian School: 6, 9} ($75.0\%$ R).
148|    \item \textbf{\texttt{v2.1 / JH11} (33 tabs, 7 sub-tasks):} Pure readout edits on \texttt{sft2000} (Idea A/C) remain at 4 coarse groups ($28.6\%$ R) because \texttt{sft2000} lacks the middle-layer representation to distinguish \emph{Kimi AI Models} from \emph{AI Decision Making}—confirming Takehi et al.'s boundary between unlocking and representation learning. \textbf{\texttt{Graft-18}} and \textbf{Idea B-1/B-2} retain all 7 fine-grained sub-task groups ($85.7\%$ P, $85.7\%$ R).
149|\end{itemize}
150|
151|\section{Conclusion}
152|Weight-space SVD and unembedding projections make LoRA post-training transparent: middle layers (L08--L15) encode cross-entity relational clustering, whereas late layers (L18--L31) govern lexical formatting and \texttt{Ungrouped} readout thresholds. Single-model neuron/scalar surgery (Ideas A, B, C) repairs over-merging and threshold overshoot in zero training steps, while dual-adapter spectral consensus (\texttt{SVD-Blend-50/50} and \texttt{Graft-18}) establishes a new Pareto frontier beyond both SFT and OPSD.
